Q: Stop &#39;run spring-boot&#39; in Intellij from opening browser window A: <p>You need to remove <code>LaunchUtil.launchBrowserInDevelopmentMode</code> to disable the automatic opening of a browser tab.</p>
<p>That is, assuming you have something like the following for your <code>SpringBootServletInitializer</code> class.</p>
<pre><code>public class Application extends SpringBootServletInitializer {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LaunchUtil.launchBrowserInDevelopmentMode(SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args));
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>You need to change it to look like the following:</p>
<pre><code>public class Application extends SpringBootServletInitializer {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }

}
</code></pre>

Q: Eclipse collection that is concurrent, has unique elements and atomic retrieval and removal A: <p>There is no collection type in Eclipse Collections that would satisfy all three characteristics, that I am aware of. There are two that satisfy the first two characteristics, with different trade-offs. This is assuming that &quot;The collection can be modified by multiple threads&quot; means thread-safe, not concurrent.</p>
<p>There is a <code>SynchronizedMutableSet</code>, which behaves similar to <code>Collections.synchronizedSet()</code> in the JDK. You can call <code>asSynchronized()</code> on any <code>MutableSet</code> to protect it in a synchronized wrapper.</p>
<pre><code>MutableSet&lt;Object&gt; synched  = Sets.mutable.empty().asSynchronized();
</code></pre>
<p>Note: <code>SynchronizedMutableSet</code> supports the same behavior as the JDK variety. This means you have to protect any calls to <code>iterator()</code> or <code>stream()</code> in a synchronized block.</p>
<p>There is a <code>MultiReaderSet</code>, which uses a <code>ReadWriteLock</code> to allow multiple readers, but only supports a single writer at a time.</p>
<pre><code>MultiReaderSet&lt;Object&gt; multiReader = Sets.multiReader.empty();
</code></pre>
<p>Note: <code>MultiReaderSet</code> fails fast on calls to <code>iterator()</code> and <code>stream()</code>, which cannot be protected without taking a read lock or write lock. Internal iteration methods on the <code>MultiReaderSet</code> that take a lambda as a parameter take a read lock automatically (e.g. <code>forEach</code>). To use <code>iterator()</code> or <code>stream()</code> safely, use either <code>withReadLockAndDelegate()</code> or <code>withWriteLockAndDelegate()</code> based on whether the code you need requires only read or potentially writes.</p>
<p>More info about Synchronized and MultiReader collections in Eclipse Collections can be found in <a href="https://medium.com/javarevisited/the-missing-java-data-structures-no-one-ever-told-you-about-part-2-f484a588cce3?source=friends_link&amp;sk=7022918ce830f2d8b94d9926e832de7d" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this blog</a>.</p>
<p>If you want access to the Eclipse Collections rich API on top of a <code>ConcurrentSkipListSet</code> or <code>CopyOnWriteArraySet</code>, you can use a <code>SetAapter</code>.</p>
<pre><code>MutableSet&lt;Object&gt; adapted1 = SetAdapter.adapt(new CopyOnWriteArraySet&lt;&gt;());
MutableSet&lt;Object&gt; adapted2 = SetAdapter.adapt(new ConcurrentSkipListSet&lt;&gt;());
</code></pre>
<p>You can also use <code>ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()</code> as an alternative, and adapt it to the Eclipse Collections <code>MutableSet</code> interface as seen below.</p>
<pre><code>Set&lt;Object&gt; keySet = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();
MutableSet&lt;Object&gt; adapted = SetAdapter.adapt(keySet);
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Update (2026-2-14):</strong> Another option is to use the method <code>Collections.newSetFromMap()</code> and adapt any concurrent map you want as a <code>Set</code>. This method was added in JDK 1.6. The <code>Set</code> that is returned can then be adapted as a <code>MutableSet</code> from EC as follows.</p>
<pre><code>Set&lt;String&gt; strings =
        Collections.newSetFromMap(new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;());
MutableSet&lt;String&gt; adapted =
        SetAdapter.adapt(strings).with(&quot;1&quot;).with(&quot;2&quot;).with(&quot;3&quot;);
Assertions.assertEquals(Set.of(&quot;1&quot;, &quot;2&quot;, &quot;3&quot;), adapted);
</code></pre>
<p>You could also implement your own <code>ConcurrentSet</code> implementation using a <code>ConcurrentMap</code> similar to how JDK <code>HashSet</code> uses <code>HashMap</code>.</p>

Q: Multi-page form validation issue with react-hook-form and arrays A: <p>If you're looking for a clean way to handle multi-step forms with react-hook-form, I built <strong>rhf-stepper</strong> <a href="https://github.com/omerrkosar/rhf-stepper" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/omerrkosar/rhf-stepper</a> for exactly this.</p>
<p>It's a headless wrapper that adds step management and per-step validation to react-hook-form — no UI opinions, works with any component library. I wrote an usage example as code if you want to integrate it to mui you can look at <a href="https://rhf-stepper-docs-git-master-omerrkosars-projects.vercel.app/docs" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://rhf-stepper-docs-git-master-omerrkosars-projects.vercel.app/docs</a> this.</p>
<pre><code>import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form'
import { Form, Step, Controller, useFormContext } from 'rhf-stepper'

function MultiStepForm() {
  const form = useForm()

  return (
    &lt;Form form={form} onSubmit={(data) =&gt; console.log(data)}&gt;
      {({ currentStep }) =&gt; (
        &lt;&gt;
          &lt;Step&gt;
            {currentStep === 0 &amp;&amp; (
              &lt;Controller name=&quot;name&quot; rules={{ required: 'Required' }}
                render={({ field }) =&gt; &lt;input {...field} /&gt;} /&gt;
            )}
          &lt;/Step&gt;
          &lt;Step&gt;
            {currentStep === 1 &amp;&amp; (
              &lt;Controller name=&quot;address&quot; rules={{ required: 'Required' }}
                render={({ field }) =&gt; &lt;input {...field} /&gt;} /&gt;
            )}
          &lt;/Step&gt;
          &lt;StepNav /&gt;
        &lt;/&gt;
      )}
    &lt;/Form&gt;
  )
}

function StepNav() {
  const { next, prev, isFirstStep, isLastStep } = useFormContext()
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      {!isFirstStep &amp;&amp; &lt;button onClick={prev}&gt;Back&lt;/button&gt;}
      {isLastStep ? &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt; : &lt;button onClick={next}&gt;Next&lt;/button&gt;}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
</code></pre>

Q: how to not display arrow in HTML &lt;details&gt; without using CSS on Safari browser A: <p>Including iOS/Safari</p>
<pre><code>&lt;style&gt;
   summary {
     list-style:none; // hides arrow for ios
   }
   summary::-webkit-details-marker {
      display:none; // hides in chrome
   }
&lt;/style&gt;
</code></pre>
